// Words the language has claimed but not implemented, refused as identifiers.
//
// doc/manual/syntax-lexical.html publishes a list of keywords that "are reserved and may
// not be used as identifiers". Three of them have no token in the lexer and so
// would compile as ordinary names; three more spell the syntax of features described in
// safe-panics.html and rec-generators.html that syntax-lexical.html has not caught up with.
// ('local' and 'wait', two more of the list, were released, Jon 28 Oct 2026: they are
// ordinary names, lexical_released_words.) Holding all six now costs a rename; letting a program bind one costs that
// program a rewrite when the feature lands. 'async', once among them, is a
// keyword now, the first word of 'async fn' (concurrency_behaviour_words), and
// so is 'yield', a generator's seam (generator_success), and 'using', a pattern
// a function runs (enum_match_using).
//
// Four names on syntax-lexical.html's list are deliberately not reserved. 'self' and
// 'this' already work, as a method's first parameter and as the value of a 'with'
// block; core_success and struct_methods pin both. 'new' is a keyword with syntax
// behind it: a construction, 'Point(1, 2)', and an init's '&new';
// struct_new pins both. 'actor' names a kind of
// declaration rather than a word with no syntax behind it, so it is a token of
// its own and the parser reports it where the declaration is written --
// trait_parse_kind_modifier. Nor are 'panic' and 'assert', which safe-panics.html
// once wrote as statements: they are core's functions, names every module has
// from core.
//
// The lexer reports the word and then releases the name, so the word behaves as
// the identifier the author meant for the rest of the compile. That is why each
// word is reported once, at its first appearance, and why the two uses of
// 'baseurl' below produce one diagnostic rather than two.
//
// This is a parse-stage file and every diagnostic in it is a parse diagnostic,
// so nothing later than the parser runs and no type-check expectation could be
// added here.

mod lexical_reject_reserved;

fn throw() { }               //~ ErrorReserved:4 "'throw' is reserved"
fn catch() { }               //~ ErrorReserved:4 "'catch' is reserved"
fn spawn() { }               //~ ErrorReserved:4 "'spawn' is reserved"

fn selfmethod() { }          //~ ErrorReserved:4 "'selfmethod' is reserved"

// A reserved word is refused wherever an identifier may appear, not only in a
// declaration's name: as a parameter, and as a local variable.
fn holder(context: i32) {     //~ ErrorReserved:11 "'context' is reserved"
  var baseurl = 2;            //~ ErrorReserved:7 "'baseurl' is reserved"
  baseurl = 3;
}
